  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 1205 PM MDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nThe long term forecast will change from having warm light winds and \nclearer skies to a more active pattern. Starting sometime Friday \nnight and through the weekend a low pressure system will start to \npush in from the Pacific Northwest and begin to start feeding that \nPacific Moisture into the Intermountain West region. THe height \ngradient will also begin to tighten so breezier conditions can also \nbe expected as the process begins to take shape. Depending on which \nglobal model you look at they all paint almost the same overall \npicture with some minute timing differences. However with weather, \nthe details and timing are always the deciding factors with how this \nunfolds. Right now, some of the global ensembles have precipitation \nstarting as early as Sunday morning some have it starting as early \nas Sunday night. Which could mean the difference of rain or snow for \nour mountain peaks and an earlier snowpack the better in my opinion. \nHowever, everywhere else in our area thats not the peaks of the \nSnowy range or Sierra Madres look to be getting rain and high \ntemperatures look to only drop into the 50's and 60's during this \nperiod.",
